
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy Policy";
}

<div class="privacy-page">

    <section class="privacy-hero">
        <span class="eyebrow">SECURITY &amp; TRANSPARENCY</span>

        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>

        <p>
            UsageMetering helps applications manage API accounts,
            user quotas, and consumption records. This policy explains
            what information the service processes, how it is used,
            and the security practices implemented by the API.
        </p>
    </section>

    <div class="privacy-content">

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>01. Information We Process</h2>

            <p>
                UsageMetering processes information required to authenticate
                accounts, enforce usage limits, and maintain consumption history.
                Depending on your interactions with the service, this may include:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Account names and application identifiers.</li>
                <li>External user identifiers associated with an account.</li>
                <li>Assigned plans, monthly allowances, and per-request limits.</li>
                <li>Usage totals for accounts and individual users.</li>
                <li>Request IDs, requested units, processing outcomes, and consumption history.</li>
                <li>API key hashes used for account authentication.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>02. How Information Is Used</h2>

            <p>
                Information is processed to provide the core functionality
                of the UsageMetering service, including:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Authenticating accounts through API keys.</li>
                <li>Associating users and custom plans with their owning accounts.</li>
                <li>Checking account-level and user-level quotas.</li>
                <li>Recording accepted and rejected consumption requests.</li>
                <li>Preventing duplicate consumption through request ID idempotency.</li>
                <li>Generating monthly usage summaries and paginated history.</li>
                <li>Supporting authorized plan management operations.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section privacy-security">
            <h2>03. API Keys &amp; Authentication</h2>

            <p>
                API keys are sensitive credentials used to authenticate
                account-scoped requests. The API stores a SHA-256 hash of
                an account's API key in PostgreSQL rather than storing the
                raw key value.
            </p>

            <p>
                Administrative plan changes use a separate administrative
                API key configured through the ADMIN_API_KEY environment
                variable. Administrative credentials must be kept secret
                and must not be committed to source control.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Never publish API keys or administrative keys in public repositories.</li>
                <li>Do not include credentials in screenshots, example payloads, or public logs.</li>
                <li>Use HTTPS for API traffic in production deployments.</li>
                <li>Use secure secret management and suitable access controls for production credentials.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                Hashing API keys does not replace the need to protect
                credentials during transmission or to secure the server,
                database, and deployment environment.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>04. Account Isolation &amp; Custom Plans</h2>

            <p>
                UsageMetering associates users, usage information, and
                custom plans with their owning accounts. Account-scoped
                API operations use the authenticated account to determine
                which resources may be accessed.
            </p>

            <p>
                Custom plans are account-owned and are not intended to be
                shared with unrelated accounts. Account owners can manage
                their own custom plans and assign eligible plans to their
                account's users, subject to the API's authorization rules.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>05. Usage Records &amp; Monthly Quotas</h2>

            <p>
                The service maintains monthly usage counters and consumption
                records to enforce account and user limits. Monthly quota
                periods use the UTC calendar month.
            </p>

            <p>
                Accepted and rejected consumption attempts are recorded.
                Rejected requests do not increment consumption counters.
                Repeated requests with the same request ID and matching
                request data do not consume units twice. Reusing a request
                ID with different request data is rejected.
            </p>

            <p>
                Historical usage summaries use stored counters for the
                requested month, but plan names and quota values reflect
                currently assigned plans rather than historical snapshots
                of plan definitions.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>06. Storage &amp; Retention</h2>

            <p>
                The service uses PostgreSQL to store account information,
                users, plan definitions, monthly usage counters,
                consumption records, and migration history.
            </p>

            <p>
                The application's database schema identifies the types
                of records maintained by the service. Actual retention
                periods depend on the database maintenance, backup,
                and deletion procedures configured by the operator.
            </p>

            <p>
                This policy does not establish a fixed deletion schedule.
                Operators should define and document retention practices
                appropriate to their deployment and applicable requirements.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>07. Deployment &amp; Third-Party Services</h2>

            <p>
                UsageMetering supports local deployment with Docker Compose
                and PostgreSQL. The README's default database credentials
                are intended for local development, not production.
            </p>

            <p>
                Production operators should configure appropriate database
                credentials, secret management, network access controls,
                and transport security. Any additional infrastructure or
                third-party services used by a deployment may have their
                own data handling practices.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>08. Data Accuracy &amp; Your Responsibilities</h2>

            <ul>
                <li>Use identifiers that do not unnecessarily expose personal information.</li>
                <li>Keep API and administrative credentials confidential.</li>
                <li>Restrict access to databases, backups, and deployment secrets.</li>
                <li>Review account access and data-retention settings regularly.</li>
                <li>Do not submit sensitive information unless it is necessary and appropriate for your use case.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="privacy-section">
            <h2>09. Policy Updates</h2>

            <p>
                This policy should be reviewed whenever the service's
                functionality, data processing, storage configuration,
                or deployment practices change. The published policy
                should always reflect the actual operation of the service.
            </p>
        </section>

    </div>

    <div class="privacy-note">
        <strong>Important:</strong>
        This policy is a project-specific starting point, not legal advice.
        Before production use, review it against the complete application,
        including the MVC frontend, API configuration, hosting environment,
        logging, backups, and applicable privacy laws.
    </div>

</div>
